diagnosis_text:
I could not reproduce a persistent network fault. End-to-end reachability from pc1 (195.11.14.2) to pc2 (200.1.1.2) shows 0% loss with low, stable RTTs. Path MTU is correct (DF pings succeed at 1500-byte frames and fail only when expected above MTU). The eBGP peering between router1 and router2 is Established with “Connections established 1; dropped 0” on both sides and the correct /24 routes installed, so the inter-router link is stable rather than flapping.

Interfaces look healthy: all links are UP/LOWER_UP with no carrier errors or RX drops. tc qdiscs on all relevant interfaces report no queue drops. An iperf from pc1→pc2 reached ~78–90 Mb/s; during that test there were 18 TCP retransmits. This correlates exactly with 18 TX drops on router2:eth1 (toward pc2), indicating brief microbursts during the stress test rather than a chronic impairment. There is no evidence of MTU blackholing, duplex mismatch, or BGP instability.

Root cause: No ongoing network fault found; brief packet loss during high-throughput testing caused transient TCP retransmissions to pc2’s segment. Corrective fix: None required. If “hiccups” recur in production, please capture timestamps and we can run longer continuous probes (e.g., ping -O/mtr) and, if needed, increase router2:eth1 txqueuelen or tune fq_codel, but current counters do not indicate a persistent issue. 
=== END ===